<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/11/03/shortcut-upload-1793717391364-0dfe9d8cf4.png" alt="Multi-window for Safari on iPhone Duo."><p class="image-caption">Multi-window for Safari on iPhone Duo.</p></div>
<p id="p2">Since Apple sent me a review unit of the iPhone Duo a couple of weeks ago, I’ve been enjoying using the device as a “pocket iPad” for getting work done with a portable, modular setup. I <a href="https://www.macstories.net/stories/macbook-pro-with-touch-a-mac-for-the-ipad-generation/" rel="noopener noreferrer">wrote this article</a> about the MacBook Pro with Touch, for instance, primarily from the iPhone Duo at Apple’s Los Angeles event – a fact that was captured in <a href="https://x.com/chancehmiller/status/2115511666956030461?a=hfDwokMIa2q_bZ2OuEQ&amp;s=46" rel="noopener noreferrer">this photo by Chance Miller</a>. (A photo, by the way, that I showed to John Ternus, who is now aware of my modded Apple kickstand.)</p>
<p id="p3">I’ll write about my experience with the iPhone Duo, productivity, and apps more and more over the next few months at MacStories. Today, I wanted to share a simple yet useful tip to easily create <strong>multiple windows for the same app</strong> on the Duo.</p>
<p id="p4"><!--more--></p>
<p id="p5">In case you’re not aware, iOS for the iPhone Duo, just like iPadOS, lets you create multiple windows for the same app. This is a feature that <a href="https://www.macstories.net/stories/ios-and-ipados-13-the-macstories-review/21/" rel="noopener noreferrer">has existed on iPad for a long time</a>, and which has gone through different permutations of window <a href="https://www.macstories.net/stories/ios-and-ipados-15-the-macstories-review/6/#the-shelf" rel="noopener noreferrer">pickers</a> and activation methods. The iPhone never had support for multi-window, and I think it makes sense to roll it out as part of the Duo’s expansive inner display alongside Split View for multitasking. The problem is that, unlike iPadOS, iOS for the Duo does not have a UI element to let you manually create a new empty window for an app that supports multiple windows. It is <em>confusing</em>: you can open multiple windows for the same app on the Duo, but you can only do so from app-specific menus that have been placed by developers in their apps’ interfaces, or from app-specific keyboard shortcuts if you’re using the Duo with an external keyboard, like I am right now.</p>
<p id="p6">In Notes, for example, you can long-press a note to ‘Open in Split View’, which will create a separate window for the selected note on the side. In <a href="https://www.goldenhillsoftware.com/unread/" rel="noopener noreferrer">Unread</a>, my favorite RSS client, there’s a custom ‘Open in New Window’ menu item that does the same for the currently selected article. These implementations are specific to each app and aren’t shared at a system-wide level on iOS 27 for the iPhone Duo. Without a menu bar or global Control Center toggle, how do you consistently create a new window for the app you’re currently using on the Duo?</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/11/03/shortcut-upload-1793717427933-70233d14df.png" alt="Multiple Safari windows in the iPhone Duo app switcher."><p class="image-caption">Multiple Safari windows in the iPhone Duo app switcher.</p></div>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/11/03/shortcut-upload-1793717489045-84f8b6bb4a.png" alt="Notes (Left) and Unread have special buttons to create new windows."><p class="image-caption">Notes (Left) and Unread have special buttons to create new windows.</p></div>
<p id="p9">Thanks to Shortcuts, I’ve landed on a solution that works pretty well as a way to create a new window in compatible apps and immediately tile it next to the current app’s existing window. Here’s how: by leveraging Shortcuts’ ‘Get Current App’ action, we can pass the currently open app as a parameter to the ‘Split Screen’ action. As it turns out, if we tell Shortcuts to <strong>split the screen between the current app and the current app</strong>, iOS will just happily create a new window for the app so the screen can be split.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/11/03/shortcut-upload-1793717515508-7c348a4430.png" alt="A simple technique to create new windows from the same app with Shortcuts."><p class="image-caption">A simple technique to create new windows from the same app with Shortcuts.</p></div>
<p id="p11">I created a shortcut for this workaround called ‘Self Split’, which you can find below and in the <a href="https://www.macstories.net/shortcuts/" rel="noopener noreferrer">MacStories Shortcuts Archive</a>. In my experience so far, the best way to use this shortcut is to add it to Control Center: this way, whenever you want to quickly create a new window for the app you’re using, you can just open Control Center, tap the shortcut, and the current app will spawn a new window on the other side of the screen.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper video-autoplay js-video-autoplay aligncenter" id="div12">
            <video data-autoplay="yes" width="1920" height="1080" data-is-retina="yes" preload="auto" playsinline controls><source src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/11/03/img-6044-1793718267360-447f336cb4.mp4" type="video/mp4"><source src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/11/03/img-6044-1793718267360-447f336cb4.webm" type="video/webm"><source src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/11/03/img-6044-1793718267360-447f336cb4.ogv" type="video/ogg"></source></source></source></video><div class="caption">
<p>Using Self Split on iPhone Duo.</p>
</div></div>
<aside class="info-box " id="aside13"><p>Remember: on iPhone Duo you don’t need to swipe down from the top-right corner of the display to open Control Center – you can just tap the Wi-Fi/battery icon instead. Also, in addition to putting a shortcut in Control Center, you could also <a href="https://support.apple.com/en-us/111775" rel="noopener noreferrer noopener noreferrer">use a mouse with AssistiveTouch</a> (the Duo doesn’t have native trackpad support…<em>yet</em>), then program one of its buttons to run a shortcut. You can do so in Settings ⇾ Accessibility ⇾ Touch ⇾ AssistiveTouch ⇾ Devices.</p>
<p class="icon"><svg viewbox="0 0 120 120" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><circle clip-rule="evenodd" cx="60" cy="60" fill-rule="evenodd" r="60"></circle><path d="m44 89v-5h13.2v-28.6h-13.1v-5h19.1v33.6h12.8v5zm10.8-53.1c0-2.8 2.2-4.9 5-4.9s5 2.1 5 4.9-2.2 5-5 5-5-2.1-5-5z" fill="#fff"></path></svg></p>
</aside><p id="p14">While this approach works for now, I’d like to see Apple continue borrowing from the work they’ve <a href="https://www.macstories.net/stories/ios-and-ipados-26-the-macstories-review/9/#the-transition-from-full-screen-split-view-and-slide-over" rel="noopener noreferrer">already done for multitasking and multi-window</a> on iPadOS and simplify it for the iPhone Duo. Just as they’ve shipped a slimmed-down, yet incredibly pleasant, version of Split View on the Duo that’s been adapted from iPadOS, so I’d like to see a new take on the <a href="https://www.macstories.net/stories/ios-and-ipados-15-the-macstories-review/6/#the-shelf" rel="noopener noreferrer">Shelf</a> or <a href="https://www.macstories.net/stories/ios-and-ipados-13-the-macstories-review/21/#expose" rel="noopener noreferrer">Exposé</a> for creating and managing multiple windows. It is a bit ironic that we’re now arguing in favor of simplifying iPad interactions for a new Duo era just like we were asking for simplified Mac features on the iPad Pro a decade ago, but here we are.</p>
<p id="p15">Until Apple improves window management on the Duo, you can get my Self Split shortcut below and find it in the <a href="https://www.macstories.net/shortcuts/" rel="noopener noreferrer">MacStories Shortcuts Archive</a>.</p>
